---
name: for-yield-layout
description: Braceless multi-line for/yield opens a layout block, in val and def-body position
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# Braceless multi-line `for … yield`

`BUGS.md` `v2-front-for-yield-parse-gap`. `for` was not a layout opener on either
self-hosted front, so `for` followed by a newline did not open a block: the indented
generator lines sat at the *enclosing* block's indent and were glued onto the `for`
line. The one-line and braced forms were never affected, because there `for` is followed
by a token rather than a newline — which is also why the label "`for … yield` is broken"
was too broad.

The shapes that already worked — they are here so a fix cannot regress them.

```scalascript
val xs = List(1, 2, 3)
val ys = List(10, 20)
println(for x <- xs yield x * 2)
println(for { a <- xs; b <- ys } yield a + b)
```

Braced multi-line, also previously fine.

```scalascript
val braced = for {
  a <- xs
  b <- ys
} yield a + b
println(braced)
```

Braceless multi-line in **`val` position**. This one produced the right answer only
because the F front declined the file and the fallback recompiled it with the default
front — a correct answer by accident, at the cost of a diagnostic on every run.

```scalascript
val single = for
  x <- xs
yield x * 2
println(single)

val double = for
  a <- xs
  b <- ys
yield a + b
println(double)
```

Braceless multi-line as a **`def` body** — the shape that failed outright, on *both*
self-hosted fronts, with `structural CoreIR contains parser sentinel _err`.

```scalascript
def pairs(): List[Int] =
  for
    a <- xs
    b <- ys
  yield a + b
println(pairs())

def doubled(): List[Int] =
  for
    x <- xs
  yield x * 2
println(doubled())
```

A braceless multi-line `for`-`do` (no `yield`) — `do` was already an opener, so this pins
that adding `for` did not disturb it.

A guard on ITS OWN LINE, both layouts. This was deliberately absent until 2026-08-11,
with the note "including it would make this gate red for a reason it does not test" —
correct at the time, and the reason is now gone: `BUGS.md`
`v2-front-for-comprehension-guard-line` is fixed, and the case that names it as its gate
could not fail on it, which is worse than naming no gate at all.

The discriminator is the SEPARATOR, not braces. `forGuard` only looked for `if`
immediately after the generator, so a newline arrived first, `forSep` read it as "another
generator follows", and the guard's boolean became a generator's collection —
`__method__: no dispatch for .map on false`. Both layouts are here because the defect was
reported in the braced form and reproduced in the braceless one.

```scalascript
val evensBraced = for {
  x <- xs
  if x % 2 == 0
} yield x
println(evensBraced)

val evensBare = for
  x <- xs
  if x % 2 == 0
yield x
println(evensBare)
```

```scalascript
for
  x <- List(7, 8)
do
  println(x)
```
